TooManyRequests.description:
Past a limit. `/problems/rate-limited`: the client's rate limit, or its workspace's limit over all its clients;
try again after Retry-After seconds (when a token is back). `/problems/quota-exceeded`: the workspace has used its
monthly quota of calls; Retry-After is the seconds until the month (UTC) ends. Nothing was done and nothing billed.

guides.markdown:
# Idempotency

Networks fail halfway: your request reached us, our answer never reached you. Was the run started? With an
`Idempotency-Key` you simply send the same request again and get the first answer back, never a second run, file or
webhook.

## The rules

- **Every `POST` that creates something requires the header** `Idempotency-Key`: 8 to 128 characters of
  `A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ : -`. A UUID is a good key. Without one the answer is 400.
- **Same key, same body, within 24 hours:** the first answer again, status and body, with `Idempotent-Replayed: true`.
  A refusal (a 4xx) is replayed too; a failure of ours (5xx) frees the key, so retrying with it is safe.
- **Same key, different body:** 409 `/problems/idempotency-conflict`. Make a new key for new work.
- **Same key while the first is still being handled:** 409 `/problems/idempotency-in-progress`; try again in a moment
  with the same key.
- Keys are your client's own: another client's key never collides with yours.

Make the key where the work is decided, store it with your own record of the work (an order, a form), and reuse it on
every retry of that work. A key per HTTP attempt defeats the purpose.

## See it

Upload the same file twice with one key: one file, the second answer replayed. Then the same key with another file:
409.

<!-- sample id="replay" expect='{"same":true,"replayed":"true","conflict":409}' -->
```bash
key=$(uuidgen)
send() {
  curl -sS -D headers.txt -o body.json "$API_URL/files" -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    -H "Idempotency-Key: $key" -F "file=@$1;type=text/csv" -w '%{http_code}'
}
printf 'order_id,amount\nORD-3001,5\n' > a.csv
printf 'order_id,amount\nORD-3002,6\n' > b.csv
send a.csv > /dev/null; first=$(jq -r .id body.json)
send a.csv > /dev/null; second=$(jq -r .id body.json)
replayed=$(grep -i '^idempotent-replayed:' headers.txt | tr -d '\r' | awk '{print $2}')
conflict=$(send b.csv)
jq -n --arg first "$first" --arg second "$second" --arg replayed "$replayed" --argjson conflict "$conflict" \
  '{first: $first, same: ($first == $second), replayed: $replayed, conflict: $conflict}'
```

```python
import json, os, uuid
import requests

url = f"{os.environ['API_URL']}/files"
headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ['TOKEN']}", "Idempotency-Key": str(uuid.uuid4())}
a = {"file": ("a.csv", b"order_id,amount\nORD-3001,5\n", "text/csv")}
b = {"file": ("b.csv", b"order_id,amount\nORD-3002,6\n", "text/csv")}

first = requests.post(url, headers=headers, files=a)
second = requests.post(url, headers=headers, files=a)
conflict = requests.post(url, headers=headers, files=b)
print(json.dumps({"first": first.json()["id"], "same": first.json()["id"] == second.json()["id"],
                  "replayed": second.headers.get("Idempotent-Replayed"), "conflict": conflict.status_code}))
```

```javascript
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';

const key = randomUUID();
const upload = text => {
  const form = new FormData();
  form.append('file', new Blob([text], { type: 'text/csv' }), 'order.csv');
  return fetch(`${process.env.API_URL}/files`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.TOKEN}`, 'Idempotency-Key': key },
    body: form,
  });
};
const first = await upload('order_id,amount\nORD-3001,5\n');
const second = await upload('order_id,amount\nORD-3001,5\n');
const conflict = await upload('order_id,amount\nORD-3002,6\n');
const [one, two] = [await first.json(), await second.json()];
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  first: one.id,
  same: one.id === two.id,
  replayed: second.headers.get('Idempotent-Replayed'),
  conflict: conflict.status,
}));
```

## What has no key

Reads (`GET`) are safe to repeat as they are. `POST /oauth/token` gives a new token each time. `POST
/runs/{runId}/view-links` stores nothing (each call answers a new link), and so takes no key.

# Rate limits and quotas

Each API client may make **600 calls a minute, with bursts of up to 100**, and all of a workspace's clients together
**1,200 a minute (burst 200)**, unless your account says otherwise. A workspace may also have a **monthly quota** of
calls (a calendar month in UTC). The token endpoint and signed links (file and view links) are not counted.

## The headers

Every counted answer says where you stand, for whichever bucket (yours or your workspace's) allows fewer calls now:

| Header | Means |
|---|---|
| `RateLimit-Limit` | The bucket's size (its burst). |
| `RateLimit-Remaining` | Calls you may make right now. |
| `RateLimit-Reset` | Seconds until the bucket is full again. |
| `RateLimit-Policy` | Its rate, e.g. `600;w=60;burst=100;scope=client`. |
| `Quota-Limit`, `Quota-Remaining`, `Quota-Reset` | With a monthly quota: its size, what is left, seconds until the month ends. |
| `Quota-Warning` | From 80% of the quota used. |

<!-- sample id="headers" expect='{"status":200}' -->
```bash
curl -sS --fail-with-body -o /dev/null -D - "$API_URL/pipelines?limit=1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  | grep -i -E '^(ratelimit|quota)-' ; echo '{"status": 200}'
```

```python
import json, os
import requests

answer = requests.get(f"{os.environ['API_URL']}/pipelines", params={"limit": 1},
                      headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ['TOKEN']}"})
answer.raise_for_status()
limits = {name: value for name, value in answer.headers.items() if name.lower().startswith(("ratelimit-", "quota-"))}
print(json.dumps({"status": answer.status_code, "limits": limits}, indent=2))
```

```javascript
const answer = await fetch(`${process.env.API_URL}/pipelines?limit=1`, {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.TOKEN}` },
});
if (!answer.ok) throw new Error(`${answer.status} ${await answer.text()}`);
const limits = Object.fromEntries([...answer.headers].filter(([name]) => /^(ratelimit|quota)-/i.test(name)));
console.log(JSON.stringify({ status: answer.status, limits }, null, 2));
```

## When you are over

Past a limit the answer is **429** with `Retry-After` (seconds), and nothing was done or billed:

- `/problems/rate-limited`: wait `Retry-After` seconds (until one call is allowed again), then carry on.
- `/problems/quota-exceeded`: the month's calls are used up; `Retry-After` is the seconds until the month (UTC) ends.
  Your account team can raise the quota.

If the limits cannot be counted for a moment, the answer is **503** with `Retry-After`: the same treatment.

## A client that waits politely

Wrap your calls once, and every call honours `Retry-After`, slows down when `RateLimit-Remaining` runs low, and never
retries forever:

<!-- sample id="polite" expect='{"status":200}' -->
```bash
call() {  # call <curl args...>: retries 429 and 503 after Retry-After, at most 5 times
  for attempt in 1 2 3 4 5; do
    status=$(curl -sS -o body.json -D headers.txt -w '%{http_code}' -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" "$@")
    if [ "$status" != 429 ] && [ "$status" != 503 ]; then cat body.json; return; fi
    wait=$(grep -i '^retry-after:' headers.txt | tr -dc '0-9'); sleep "${wait:-1}"
  done
  cat body.json; return 1
}
call "$API_URL/pipelines?limit=1" > /dev/null && echo '{"status": 200}'
```

```python
import json, os, time
import requests


def call(method, url, **kwargs):
    """One API call; 429 and 503 wait Retry-After and try again, at most 5 times."""
    headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {os.environ['TOKEN']}", **kwargs.pop("headers", {})}
    for attempt in range(5):
        answer = requests.request(method, url, headers=headers, **kwargs)
        if answer.status_code not in (429, 503):
            if answer.headers.get("RateLimit-Remaining") == "0":
                time.sleep(int(answer.headers.get("RateLimit-Reset", "1")))
            return answer
        time.sleep(int(answer.headers.get("Retry-After", "1")))
    return answer


answer = call("GET", f"{os.environ['API_URL']}/pipelines", params={"limit": 1})
answer.raise_for_status()
print(json.dumps({"status": answer.status_code}))
```

```javascript
const sleep = seconds => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, seconds * 1000));

/** One API call; 429 and 503 wait Retry-After and try again, at most 5 times. */
async function call(url, init = {}) {
  let answer;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 5; attempt++) {
    answer = await fetch(url, { ...init, headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.TOKEN}`, ...init.headers } });
    if (answer.status !== 429 && answer.status !== 503) {
      if (answer.headers.get('RateLimit-Remaining') === '0') await sleep(Number(answer.headers.get('RateLimit-Reset') ?? 1));
      return answer;
    }
    await sleep(Number(answer.headers.get('Retry-After') ?? 1));
  }
  return answer;
}

const answer = await call(`${process.env.API_URL}/pipelines?limit=1`);
if (!answer.ok) throw new Error(`${answer.status} ${await answer.text()}`);
console.log(JSON.stringify({ status: answer.status }));
```

A `POST` retried this way keeps its `Idempotency-Key` (it is in the headers you pass), so a retry never does the work
twice.

## Billing

Each counted call is billed as `api.calls`, and each webhook delivery that reaches your receiver as
`webhook.deliveries`, per 1,000 on your workspace's rate card. Nothing in a sandbox is billed.
